Good evening. This is India Markets Brief from toro I-Q. Your read on Thursday's session.

More than half of Thursday's fall in the Nifty was one share. H-D-F-C Bank closed down two point two three percent, and on about thirteen percent of the index that is most of a decline of zero point four eight percent. The rest of the banks were fine.

So this is not a banking story. What is wearing H-D-F-C Bank down is its own arithmetic. Its lending margin is at a record low, its cheap deposits have shrunk from thirty eight percent of the book to thirty two in two years, and foreign investors have cut their holding for a fifth straight quarter.

The Nifty fifty closed at twenty four thousand and ninety. Underneath it, of seven hundred and seven shares we could price, two hundred and fifty three rose and four hundred and forty nine fell, and the typical share lost zero point four six percent.

The selling was concentrated rather than general. Not one of eleven cement companies rose. Metals were next, then state owned banks, with exactly one gainer in twelve. The gold lenders were sold as a group, because gold is lower and they can only lend against a fraction of the gold pledged with them. Pharmaceuticals were the one clean positive, up zero point eight four percent.

Asia split on Thursday and India went with the weaker half. Korea, Shanghai and Taiwan rose. Japan and Hong Kong fell.

Two things sit over this market and they disagree. The Reserve Bank of India's August bulletin, out on Tuesday, reads the June quarter as resilient, with exports and imports both growing in double digits in July, which cuts against the idea that the eighteen percent American tariff is crushing exports. But rainfall through the twenty sixth of August is thirteen percent below normal, and that shortfall has widened since early August.

Both are true. The bulletin describes July, when sowing had recovered, and the deficit widened after that. Thursday's market sided with the rain, with fertiliser, agrochemical and rural facing names lower. The direction lines up, and we are not calling it the cause.
